# Java Enum

An `enum` in Java is used when we have a **fixed set of constant values**.

For example, suppose a student can have only these statuses:

```text
ACTIVE
INACTIVE
GRADUATED
```

Instead of storing them as random strings, we can use an `enum`.

---

# 1. What is an Enum?

`enum` stands for **enumeration**.

It allows us to define a fixed group of values.

```java
enum Status {
    ACTIVE,
    INACTIVE,
    GRADUATED
}
```

Now `Status` can only represent these defined values.

---

# 2. Using an Enum

```java
enum Status {
    ACTIVE,
    INACTIVE,
    GRADUATED
}

class Student {

    String name;
    Status status;
}
```

Create an object:

```java
Student student = new Student();

student.name = "Nikitha";
student.status = Status.ACTIVE;
```

Now:

```java
System.out.println(student.status);
```

Output:

```text
ACTIVE
```

---

# 3. Why Not Just Use Strings?

We could do:

```java
String status = "ACTIVE";
```

But someone could accidentally write:

```java
status = "Actve";
```

or:

```java
status = "Something";
```

Java won't stop us because they are just strings.

With an enum:

```java
Status status = Status.ACTIVE;
```

only the defined values can be used.

```text
Status
 ├── ACTIVE
 ├── INACTIVE
 └── GRADUATED
```

This makes the code safer and easier to understand.

---

# 4. Enum with `switch`

Enums work very well with `switch`.

```java
enum Status {
    ACTIVE,
    INACTIVE,
    GRADUATED
}
```

Then:

```java
Status status = Status.ACTIVE;

switch (status) {

    case ACTIVE:
        System.out.println("Student is active");
        break;

    case INACTIVE:
        System.out.println("Student is inactive");
        break;

    case GRADUATED:
        System.out.println("Student has graduated");
        break;
}
```

Output:

```text
Student is active
```

---

# 5. Getting All Enum Values

We can use:

```java
Status.values()
```

Example:

```java
for (Status status : Status.values()) {
    System.out.println(status);
}
```

Output:

```text
ACTIVE
INACTIVE
GRADUATED
```

This is useful when you want to work with every possible enum value.

---

# 6. Getting the Enum Name

Every enum value has a name.

```java
Status status = Status.ACTIVE;

System.out.println(status.name());
```

Output:

```text
ACTIVE
```

---

# 7. Enum `valueOf()`

We can convert a string into an enum value using `valueOf()`.

```java
Status status = Status.valueOf("ACTIVE");

System.out.println(status);
```

Output:

```text
ACTIVE
```

The string must match the enum constant exactly.

For example:

```java
Status.valueOf("active");
```

is different from:

```java
Status.valueOf("ACTIVE");
```

and will throw an exception if `"active"` is not defined.

---

# 8. Enum with Numbers

Every enum constant has an ordinal position.

```java
enum Level {
    LOW,
    MEDIUM,
    HIGH
}
```

We can use:

```java
System.out.println(Level.LOW.ordinal());
System.out.println(Level.MEDIUM.ordinal());
System.out.println(Level.HIGH.ordinal());
```

Output:

```text
0
1
2
```

The numbering starts from `0`.

However, don't use `ordinal()` as a permanent database ID because changing the enum order can change the ordinal values.

---

# 9. Enum Can Have Methods

An enum is more powerful than just a list of names.

It can contain methods.

```java
enum Level {

    LOW,
    MEDIUM,
    HIGH;

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Level: " + this);
    }
}
```

Use it:

```java
Level.HIGH.display();
```

Output:

```text
Level: HIGH
```

---

# 10. Enum Can Have Variables

An enum can also contain fields.

```java
enum Level {

    LOW(1),
    MEDIUM(2),
    HIGH(3);

    int value;

    Level(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }
}
```

Now:

```java
System.out.println(Level.HIGH.value);
```

Output:

```text
3
```

Here each enum constant has its own value.

```text
LOW     → 1
MEDIUM  → 2
HIGH    → 3
```

