# 11. Real-World Example

Enums are useful when a value has a fixed set of possibilities.

### Order status

```java
enum OrderStatus {
    PLACED,
    SHIPPED,
    DELIVERED,
    CANCELLED
}
```

### User role

```java
enum Role {
    ADMIN,
    USER,
    MANAGER
}
```

### Payment status

```java
enum PaymentStatus {
    PENDING,
    SUCCESS,
    FAILED
}
```

### Days

```java
enum Day {
    MONDAY,
    TUESDAY,
    WEDNESDAY,
    THURSDAY,
    FRIDAY,
    SATURDAY,
    SUNDAY
}
```

---

# 12. Enum vs String

| String | Enum |
|---|---|
| Can contain any text | Fixed set of values |
| Easy to make spelling mistakes | Safer |
| No fixed choices | Fixed choices |
| `"ACTIVE"` and `"active"` are different | Constants are predefined |
| Less structured | More structured |

---

# 13. Easy Mental Model

Think of an enum as a **menu of allowed choices**.

```text
OrderStatus
     │
     ├── PLACED
     ├── SHIPPED
     ├── DELIVERED
     └── CANCELLED
```

You cannot randomly add:

```text
"COOKING"
```

unless it is defined inside the enum.

---

# 14. Complete Example

```java
enum PaymentStatus {
    PENDING,
    SUCCESS,
    FAILED
}

class Payment {

    PaymentStatus status;

    Payment(PaymentStatus status) {
        this.status = status;
    }

    void display() {

        switch (status) {

            case PENDING:
                System.out.println("Payment is pending");
                break;

            case SUCCESS:
                System.out.println("Payment successful");
                break;

            case FAILED:
                System.out.println("Payment failed");
                break;
        }
    }
}
```

Use it:

```java
Payment payment =
        new Payment(PaymentStatus.SUCCESS);

payment.display();
```

Output:

```text
Payment successful
```

---

# Key Takeaways

- `enum` represents a **fixed set of values**.
- Enum constants are predefined.
- Enums provide more safety than using random strings.
- Enums work well with `switch`.
- `values()` returns all enum constants.
- `valueOf()` converts a matching string into an enum value.
- Enums can have constructors, fields, and methods.
- Common uses include statuses, roles, categories, days, and fixed choices.

---

## One-Line Summary

> **An enum is a Java type used to represent a fixed and well-defined set of constant values.**
